   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The CEACR in its Observation adopted in 2022 on the Worst Forms of Child Labour Convention , 1999 (No.182) noted with satisfaction that section 66(3) of the Act Relating to Children, 2018 deals with offenses related to child sexual abuse and makes it an offence to use a child for the production of any obscene material or act; to engage or cause to engage a child in sexual exploitation or to use or cause to use a child in sexual abuse, including child pornography, and offences are punishable with a fine of up to 100,000 rupees and imprisonment for up to five years. The ILO has been working with its constituents in Nepal for the elimination of the worst forms of child labour for the last few decades. Building on the support of the governments of Denmark and the United Kingdom in the previous biennium, the ILO strengthened national capacity, raised awareness and supported policy responses to eliminate the worst forms of child labour through credible data and knowledge bases as well as the implementation of various interventions to enhance knowledge of policy makers on international labour standards on child labour. The ILO, in collaboration with the Central Bureau of Statistics, published the Nepal Child Labour Report (2011). The ILO, together with UNICEF, World Education, the Ministry of Labour, Employment, and Social Security, and the National Human Rights Commission, published a summary report on child labour in Nepal in 2017, including the worst forms of child labour. These reports provided data, highlighted sexual exploitation of children as one of the major problems in Nepal, and urged the concerned stakeholders to take the necessary actions.

Furthermore, the ILO organised training for trainers on the elimination of the worst forms of child labour in Nepal in partnership with Nepal Administration College from January to June 2015. A pool of 50 National Master Trainers comprised government officials, including from the Ministry of Labour, Employment and Social Security , the Ministry of Women, Children and Senior Citizens (the custodian ministry for the Children Act 2018), workers&apos; and employers&apos; representatives, and representatives of civil society organisations, who later advocated for addressing the worst forms of child labour, especially sexual exploitation of children, through participating in various meetings organised by the Ministry of Women in relation to the revision of the Children Act 2018.</narrative>
   </description>
